This is a complete guide to building a full-stack application using **React** (Frontend), **Node.js**, and **Express.js** (Backend) with an in-memory database (to keep it simple without installing MongoDB or SQL right away).

We will build an **"Employee Management System"** where you can:
1.  Create dummy employees.
2.  List all employees.
3.  Delete employees.

Finally, I will explain how to deploy this to the cloud (Render/Heroku) or locally as a production build.

---

### Part 1: Project Structure

Create a main folder named `my-app`. Inside, create two folders: `client` (React) and `server` (Node).

```text
my-app/
├── client/   (React App)
└── server/   (Express API)
```

---

### Part 2: The Backend (Node & Express)

1.  **Initialize the server:**
    Open your terminal, go to the `server` folder, and run:
    ```bash
    cd server
    npm init -y
    npm install express cors
    ```

2.  **Create `server/index.js`:**
    This file handles the API logic and stores dummy data in RAM.

    ```javascript
    // server/index.js
    const express = require('express');
    const cors = require('cors');
    const app = express();

    // Middleware
    app.use(cors()); // Allow React to talk to Node
    app.use(express.json());

    // Mock Database (In-Memory)
    let employees = [
        { id: 1, name: 'John Doe', role: 'Developer', email: 'john@example.com' },
        { id: 2, name: 'Jane Smith', role: 'Designer', email: 'jane@example.com' },
        { id: 3, name: 'Mike Ross', role: 'Manager', email: 'mike@example.com' }
    ];

    let nextId = 4;

    // API Routes

    // 1. Get all employees
    app.get('/api/employees', (req, res) => {
        res.json(employees);
    });

    // 2. Create a new employee
    app.post('/api/employees', (req, res) => {
        const { name, role, email } = req.body;
        const newEmployee = { id: nextId++, name, role, email };
        employees.push(newEmployee);
        res.status(201).json(newEmployee);
    });

    // 3. Delete an employee
    app.delete('/api/employees/:id', (req, res) => {
        const { id } = req.params;
        employees = employees.filter(emp => emp.id !== parseInt(id));
        res.status(200).json({ message: 'Deleted successfully' });
    });

    // Start Server
    const PORT = 5000;
    app.listen(PORT, () => {
        console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
    });
    ```

